      "sample_index": 4,
      "text": "To find the mean value of the measures of the three interior angles of any triangle, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Sum of Interior Angles of a Triangle:**\n   The sum of the interior angles of any triangle is always \\(180^\\circ\\). This is a fundamental property of triangles.\n\n2. **Mean Value Calculation:**\n   The mean value of a set of numbers is calculated by summing all the values and then dividing by the number of values. In this case, we have three angles (let's call them \\(A\\), \\(B\\), and \\(C\\)).\n\n   The formula for the mean value is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Mean} = \\frac{A + B + C}{3}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Substitute the Sum of Angles:**\n   Since the sum of the interior angles of a triangle is \\(180^\\circ\\), we can substitute \\(A + B + C = 180^\\circ\\) into the formula:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Mean} = \\frac{180^\\circ}{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Perform the Division:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{Mean} = 60^\\circ\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the mean value of the measures of the three interior angles of any triangle is \\(\\boxed{60^\\circ}\\).",
